// `std::conventions::screenplay` — the built-in screenplay preset
// (issue #1720, Track 1 step 8 of #1351).
//
// `docs/prose-dialect-spec.md` §3.4/§3.5: "Presets ship as modules
// (`std::conventions::screenplay`)... The producer compiles and evaluates
// it (existing machinery: the compiler + `begin_function_eval`) and
// freezes the resulting *value* into the `Environment` — the compiler
// never consumes the module, only the value." §9.1's 2026-07-31 ruling
// ("Conventions are annotated handlers") dissolved the `Conventions` type
// and the `lower:` column this file used to need: a preset element is
// literally an annotated handler — matched line, named captures bound to
// params by name, exactly one call — the same three steps `!name`
// dispatch takes, minus the sigil.
//
// ⚠ STATUS (honest, as of this issue): this file is the preset's SOURCE
// FORM, matching §3.5's authored shape. It is NOT YET REACHABLE via
// `use std::conventions::screenplay` in any real project, for two
// independent, already-tracked reasons:
//
//   1. No `std::`-namespaced module resolution exists in the compiler at
//      all. Native source discovery is "tree-is-universe" (every `.brink`
//      file under a project's root joins that project — see
//      `brink-driver`'s discovery, CLAUDE.md's own note) with no concept
//      of an importable standard-library path; the closest existing
//      precedent, the T1b stdlib slice, is Rust-side bare-name
//      recognition (`brink_analyzer::resolve::is_t1b_stdlib_name`), not a
//      real `.brink` source file anyone imports.
//   2. `fn conventions()` registration + comptime evaluation (issue
//      #1840, "Conventions v1c") has not landed — `register(...)` itself
//      has no resolvable meaning yet (verified against `origin/main` at
//      the time of this issue: `register` is an unresolved-variable
//      `E025`, not a builtin). Per the 2026-08-01 side ruling this
//      issue's briefing carries: this preset's resolved projection is
//      meant to be PRE-FROZEN and shipped, so a DEFAULT project pays
//      ZERO comptime — only a project that OVERRIDES the preset would
//      ever evaluate anything. This file is written so that pre-freezing
//      is possible once #1840 lands (every handler below is a plain,
//      side-effect-free rewrite — no state, no externals, nothing a
//      `pure`/`register`-effect evaluator couldn't freeze) — it does not
//      attempt to build the freezing/evaluation machinery itself, which
//      would require running the evaluator for every default project and
//      violate that same ruling.
//
// **What IS proven end to end today**: every handler below is byte-for-
// byte the same shape as `tests/tier1-native/conventions-screenplay-
// preset/story.brink`, a project that inlines these exact declarations
// (the only reachable way to exercise a claiming handler before #1840 —
// single-file `@[element(claims = …)]` dispatch, issues #1838/#1839) and
// pins the resulting transcript against a real compile + run. That test
// is this preset's regression coverage; keep the two in sync.
//
// # Element inventory covered (docs/prose-dialect-spec.md §8, §8b, §8d)
//
// - `heading` — scene headings (`INT.`/`EXT.` prefix, §3.3/§8b.7). Scene
//   entry is ruled a lowered host call (`scene_entered(title, slug)`),
//   but no such extern exists to call yet (this preset does not invent
//   one) — this handler emits the announcement as plain text instead,
//   which is as far as "elements reach runtime output" goes before
//   #1683/#1684 (`Line`/`Step` carry no structured element metadata, only
//   text a handler body emits). **Only claims a heading with NO explicit
//   `[slug]` and no trailing tags** — `hir::lower_native::element::
//   candidate`'s literalness rule declines a heading carrying either,
//   the same way it declines a `CONTENT_LINE` carrying interpolation;
//   every worked-page heading in the spec (`[market]`, `[chapel_found]`,
//   `[alley_escape]`) uses an explicit slug and so is NOT claimable by
//   this handler as written. Promoting a heading to a real HIR stitch (a
//   genuine divert target, §3.2/§3.3's "a scene heading may declare a
//   stitch") is not built anywhere in the compiler — issue #1717, which
//   would have owned that, was closed as superseded by the §9.1 ruling
//   without delivering it. So `-> chapel_found`-style diverts into a
//   heading-declared stitch, as §8c's worked case writes them, are not
//   reachable through any preset; a project wanting that today still
//   needs an ordinary `flow chapel_found() { … }`.
// - `transition` — a bare, all-caps line ending in `:` (`CUT TO:`,
//   `SMASH CUT TO:`, §8b.7/§8d). Re-emits its own text verbatim; diverts
//   stay absolutely invisible either way (RULED, unconditionally — a
//   transition dresses the divert that follows, it never touches it).
// - `cue` — a bare `@NAME` speaker line (§8b.9/§8d.4), `block`-capturing
//   the dialogue that follows it directly. Only claims a cue with no
//   trailing tag extension (`@VENDOR #(v.o.)` declines the same way a
//   slug-carrying heading does — the tag is structure the pattern is
//   never shown).
// - `parenthetical` — a chain-gated `(delivery)` line (parser-enforced:
//   only recognized directly after a live cue), `block`-capturing the
//   dialogue that follows IT.
//
// ⚠ **Cue + parenthetical + dialogue is two independent claims, not one
// joined attachment.** `capture_block`'s ruled terminator (issue #1839)
// ends a captured run at "a blank line, or any element-level line" —
// and a `PARENTHETICAL` is explicitly one of those. So when a cue is
// directly followed by a parenthetical (the common screenplay shape,
// `docs/prose-dialect-spec.md` §8's own worked pages: `@VENDOR` /
// `(hushed)` / dialogue), `cue`'s own `block` capture sees ZERO lines —
// the very next item is the parenthetical, not plain dialogue — and
// `cue` emits the speaker name followed by a BLANK line (the `>{ {name}
// \n{body} }` template's own newline still renders even when `{body}`
// interpolates to the empty `Fragment` — pinned in `tests/tier1-native/
// conventions-screenplay-preset/expected.txt`). `parenthetical` is then claimed on
// its own very next iteration, and IT captures the dialogue. There is no
// context injection (deferred, §3.5b's own Deferred list: "handlers
// reading attachment data") linking the two calls — `cue` never learns
// what `parenthetical` captured, and vice versa. This is not a bug this
// preset works around; it is what the currently-landed mechanism
// supports, and `crates/internal/brink-ir/src/hir/lower_native/tests.rs`
// (`a_cue_block_capture_stops_at_a_following_parenthetical_and_the_
// parenthetical_claims_separately`) pins exactly this split.
//
// ⚠ **`try_claim` matches purely on extracted TEXT against a handler's
// pattern — it never restricts a handler to only the grammar kind its
// author had in mind.** `candidate()` decides whether a node offers
// literal text to match at all; it does not gate WHICH handler may claim
// it. A permissive `parenthetical` pattern (the obvious `.+`) would also
// re-claim the very dialogue it just captured, the moment `capture_block`
// re-lowers that captured run through the ordinary dispatch loop — proven
// while building this preset (an earlier draft used `.+` and produced a
// THIRD, spurious `parenthetical` call over "You shouldn't be here.").
// `parenthetical`'s pattern below is therefore deliberately narrow
// (lowercase-initial only, no punctuation) — real screenplay
// parenthetical deliveries (`(hushed)`, `(beat)`, `(to Kid)` excepted —
// capitalized names inside a delivery are NOT covered by this pattern,
// a known, documented narrowing) satisfy this; ordinary dialogue
// (capitalized, punctuated) does not. Widening this pattern safely needs
// either a kind-aware dispatch restriction (a mechanism change beyond
// this issue's fence) or a token that survives the parens/braces some
// other way — not attempted here.
//
// ⚠ **This is not just a self-re-claim hazard — it is the real constraint
// on this preset's patterns.** Because `try_claim` is kind-blind (see
// above: `candidate()` gates which NODE KINDS offer text to match, not
// which handler may claim a given match), `parenthetical`'s
// `^[a-z][a-z' -]*$` pattern will claim ANY all-lowercase,
// punctuation-free line anywhere a `CONTENT_LINE` is itself a claim
// candidate — not only a chain-gated `PARENTHETICAL` node and not only
// inside dialogue. An ordinary, unrelated action/prose line elsewhere in
// the same project (`she waits`, `he nods slowly`) matches the same
// pattern and gets wrongly rewritten through `parenthetical`, block-
// capturing whatever plain lines follow it. Keeping every ordinary prose
// line either capitalized, punctuated, or otherwise outside this exact
// shape is the only guard today — there is no kind restriction to fall
// back on.
//
// ⚠ **Compact cues (`@NAME: text`, §8b.9) and any cue/heading carrying a
// tag extension are NOT covered.** `COMPACT_CUE` fuses a `CUE_NAME` and
// an ordinary `CONTENT_LINE` (which may itself carry markup) into one
// node; `candidate()` widens to `CUE`/`PARENTHETICAL` for this issue but
// deliberately not to `COMPACT_CUE`, since matching it safely needs
// either a two-piece capture (name AND fused text) `try_claim`'s
// single-text-node contract does not support today, or a policy for
// what happens when the fused line is not wholly literal. Left
// unclaimed — a compact cue still reports the pre-existing loud `E129`,
// never silently read as ordinary prose.
//
// # `@[style]` — deliberately NOT declared here
//
// `docs/prose-dialect-spec.md` §3.5b names the screenplay preset as the
// example that would make itself self-describing via `@[style(... =
// "[right, uppercase]")]` (transitions right-aligned + uppercase, cues
// uppercase). That section's own Deferred list states plainly that
// **multi-token style values are not built**: a `key = "a b"` value
// lowers to one `StyleToken::Custom("a b")`, not two tokens — so writing
// `[right, uppercase]` here would silently ship a decorative annotation
// that lies about what it does. Left off entirely rather than shipping
// a half-working one; add it once multi-token values land.

@[element(claims = "^(?<kind>INT|EXT)\\. (?<title>.+)$")]
fn heading(kind: string, title: string) {
  return "-- {kind}. {title} --";
}

@[element(claims = "^(?<text>[A-Z][A-Z '-]*:)$")]
fn transition(text: string) {
  return text;
}

@[element(claims = "^(?<name>[A-Z][A-Z '-]*)$", block)]
fn cue(name: string, body: content) >{
  {name}
  {body}
}

@[element(claims = "^(?<delivery>[a-z][a-z' -]*)$", block)]
fn parenthetical(delivery: string, body: content) >{
  ({delivery})
  {body}
}
